For full nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states, $$\label{eq:joint-limit}
 \frac{I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}}\longrightarrow a_{\mathrm{TF}}>0
 \quad\text{whenever}\quad m\longrightarrow\infty,
 \qquad \frac Zm\longrightarrow\infty,$$ where $Z>m\ge1$ are integers. In particular, $$\begin{aligned}
 \lim_{m\to\infty}
  \frac{\limsup_{Z\to\infty} I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}}
   &=a_{\mathrm{TF}},\label{eq:iterated-upper}\\
 \lim_{m\to\infty}
  \frac{\liminf_{Z\to\infty} I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}}
   &=a_{\mathrm{TF}}.\label{eq:iterated-lower}
\end{aligned}$$ The inner limits are taken over integer $Z$ with $m$ fixed. No convergence of $I_m(Z)$ at a fixed $m$ is asserted or assumed.

The sequence $E_n$ is nonincreasing. If $E_n<E_{n-1}$, there is a normalized ground state in sector $n$, and $n\le2Z+1\le3Z$. There is a largest such index $N_*$, with $Z\le N_*\le3Z$, and $$E=E_{N_*}=E_n\quad(n\ge N_*).$$ There are universal $C,Z_0$ such that $$\label{eq:neutral-offset}
 E_Z(Z)\le E+C\qquad(Z\ge Z_0).$$ If $\Psi$ is a normalized sector ground state and $F$ is a bounded real symmetric Lipschitz function of the spatial configuration, then $$\label{eq:ground-multiplier}
 \langle F\Psi,H_{Z,n}F\Psi\rangle
       -E_n\|F\Psi\|_2^2
       =\frac12\mathbb E_\Psi|\nabla F|^2,$$ where the left side is interpreted as a quadratic form and the gradient includes all spatial coordinates. Finally, every unshifted $\delta$-state in a sector $n\le3Z$ satisfies $$\label{eq:global-density-input}
 \int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho_\psi^{5/3}\le C(Z^{7/3}+\delta).$$

Let $\psi$ be a normalized unshifted $\delta$-state of finite particle number. For fixed $0<\alpha<\beta<\infty$ and every $u>0$, $$\label{eq:annular-count}
 \left\|\#\{i:\alpha u\le|x_i|\le\beta u\}\right\|_2
   \le C_{\alpha,\beta}
           \bigl(\max(u^{-3},1)+\sqrt{\delta u}\bigr).$$ Suppose in addition that $n\le3Z$, and set $$B_{u/8}(x)=V(x)-\sum_{|x_i|<u/8}\frac1{|x-x_i|}.$$ Let $Y$ be trivial or be the data of a square cut whose out support lies in $\{u/4\le|x|\le8u\}$ and whose squared gradient sum is at most $C/u^2$. There are conditional versions harmonic on $u/4<|x|<6u$ for which $$\label{eq:conditional-inner-field}
 \left\|\sup_{u/2\le|x|\le4u}
              \bigl(\mathbb E[B_{u/8}(x)\mid Y]\bigr)_+\right\|_2
 \le C\left(\frac{\max(u^{-3},1)}u+\sqrt{\frac\delta u}\right).$$ The constant in the second estimate may depend on the fixed gradient bound. Both estimates allow every finite $\delta$.

There is a universal constant $C_{\mathrm{cap}}$ with the following property. Fix $$M\geq2,\qquad 0<h_l<h_h<\infty,\qquad 0<\xi<h_l/16.$$ For all sufficiently far systems along <a href="#eq:scaling-regime" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:scaling-regime">[eq:scaling-regime]</a>, at every $j<J_*$ there is an event $\mathcal G_j\in\mathcal F_j$ such that, with $r=r_j$, $$\mathbb P(\mathcal G_j^c)\leq Cr^{25}.$$ On $\mathcal G_j$, the following assertions hold simultaneously for $r\leq d_y\leq4Mr$ and $h_l\leq h\leq h_h$: $$\begin{split}
 d_y^4H'_j(y)&\leq C_{\mathrm{cap}},\\
 d_y^6\mu_j(y)>kh^{3/2}
       &\quad\Longrightarrow\quad d_y^4H'_j(y)\geq h-\xi,\\
 d_y^6\mu_j(y)<kh^{3/2}
       &\quad\Longrightarrow\quad d_y^4H'_j(y)\leq h+\xi.
 \end{split}
 \label{eq:inverse-comparison}$$ The probability constant and the threshold for sufficiently far systems may depend on the displayed fixed parameters and on the fixed packet conventions; $C_{\mathrm{cap}}$ is independent of the band and accuracy parameters.

There are fixed positive constants $\varepsilon,B,C_{\mathrm{inv}}$ and $M>2$ with the following property. For every sufficiently far system in <a href="#eq:scaling-regime" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:scaling-regime">[eq:scaling-regime]</a>, use the observations and kernels of [eq:observation-scales,eq:master-kernel] [eq:observation-scales](#eq:observation-scales), [eq:master-kernel](#eq:master-kernel) with this $\varepsilon$. For every $0\le j\le J_*$ there exist $\mathcal F_j$-measurable functions $u_j,p_j$ such that, with $r=r_j$, $$\label{eq:barrier-invariant}
 \begin{aligned}
 \Delta u_j&\ge-4\pi\nu
       +4\pi\mathbf 1_{\{d<r\}}\mu_j-4\pi p_j
       +\mathbf 1_{\{d\ge r\}}f(u_j),\\
 b_r\le u_j&\le C_{\mathrm{inv}}d^{-4}\quad(d\ge r),\\
 u_j&=b_r\quad(d>Mr).
 \end{aligned}$$ The first inequality holds in distributions on $\mathbb R^3$. For each fixed system, $u_j-V$ is continuous and locally Lipschitz, with deterministic bounds on compact sets; $p_j$ is a nonnegative bounded density supported in $\{d\le r_j\}$, also with a deterministic bound. Its expected total mass satisfies $$\label{eq:barrier-error-mass}
 \sup_{0\le j\le J_*}\mathbb E\int p_j
 \le C\bigl(r_0^{32}+s^{19/2-3w}\bigr)=o(1).$$ The deterministic fixed-system regularity bounds need not be uniform in $Z$; the constants in <a href="#eq:barrier-error-mass" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:barrier-error-mass">[eq:barrier-error-mass]</a> are uniform.

There is at most one classical solution $F$ of $$\Delta F=4\pi k\bigl((F-1)_+\bigr)^{3/2}
       \quad\text{on }\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}$$ which tends uniformly to zero at infinity and satisfies $c|x|^{-4}\le F(x)\le C|x|^{-4}$ near zero for some $c,C>0$. Every such solution has the uniform singular asymptotic $$\label{eq:sommerfeld-coefficient}
 |x|^4F(x)\longrightarrow
 A_{\mathrm S}:=\left(\frac{12}{4\pi k}\right)^2
       \qquad(|x|\downarrow0).$$ If a solution exists, it is radial.

Let $q$ be the deficit coefficient in <a href="#thm:priced-deficit" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:priced-deficit">2</a>. For every sequence of integers $Z>m\ge1$ such that $m\to\infty$ and $Z/m\to\infty$, $$\label{eq:fixed-sector-limit}
 \frac{E_Z(Z-m)-E_Z(Z)}{m^{7/3}}
       \longrightarrow\frac37q^{-4/3}.$$

With the two-spin coefficient $T$ fixed above, $$\label{eq:tf-unit-deficit}
 \lim_{Z\to\infty}
       \bigl[E_Z^{\mathrm{TF}}(Z-1)-E_Z^{\mathrm{TF}}(Z)\bigr]
       =\frac37q^{-4/3}.$$ The limit holds through real charges $Z>1$. Consequently <a href="#eq:tf-constant" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:tf-constant">[eq:tf-constant]</a> holds for every fixed real $m>0$, with $$a_{\mathrm{TF}}=\frac37q^{-4/3}>0.$$
